  "note": "Issue #401: interceptor_crimson and interceptor_sand are LAN guests' liveries (content/core/flow/lan.json): the locals' recolour with another band. Issue #76: a look with 'darken' { body_mul, accent_mul, glow } shows its model through the dark-gun shader (weapon_dark.gdshader): the Remnant's dark interceptor and freighter. Ship looks, keyed by the 'look' id in content/core/ships/*.json. Shapes for the stand-ins live in src/systems/look/ship_kit.gd; colours and sizes live here. To use a real model, set 'scene' to a .tscn/.glb path (forward = -Z, 1 unit = 1 metre); the stand-in is then skipped. See docs/ADDING_SHIPS.md. Issue #43: the four flyable ships (interceptor, freighter, courier, gunship) use Hyper3D models from assets/vehicles/ (content/look/vehicle_models.json, tools/look/prep_vehicle.gd); their stand-in fields stay as the fallback. 'flames_at' [[x, y, z, radius], ...] puts an engine flame (lit by the throttle, ShipKit.throttle_flames) at each of a model's nozzles, pointing aft (+Z); a stand-in gets one at each engine glow. 'flame_length' (default 6) is a flame's length in nozzle radii.",
  "hull_fit": {
    "note": "How a ship's collision hull follows its model (ShipHull.fit, src/systems/flight/ship_hull.gd): the mesh is cut into a grid of cells about 'cell' metres across (at most 'max_cells' a side) and each cell's points wrapped in a convex hull, with 'overlap' metres shared across cell walls so the pieces meet; at most 'max_points' per cell go into a hull. A mesh of no more than 'part_verts' vertices (a stand-in's box or cylinder, a bolted-on part) is convex already and keeps its own hull. A flat piece is thickened to 'min_thickness'. Smaller cells follow the shape closer and cost more pieces.",
    "cell": 1.2,
